# KachnaDocs environment configuration.
# Copy to .env in this same directory (git-ignored) and fill in.
#
# Who reads it:
#   - The container: `docker run --env-file .env`, or `env_file: .env` in a
#     compose file. The image ships no defaults of its own, so everything under
#     "Required" has to come from here (or from the shell/proxying layer).
#   - Local dev instead reads apps/api/.env, a separate git-ignored file next to
#     the API source. See the "Development" section at the bottom.
#
# A real exported environment variable always outranks this file — dotenv never
# overwrites what the process already has — so a deployed value can't be shadowed
# by a stale local copy. That also means a one-off
# `SESSION_SECRET=... docker compose up` wins over whatever is written here.
#
# Values below are deliberately unquoted. Compose's dotenv parser strips matching
# quotes around a value, but `docker run --env-file` does not — it passes them
# through literally, and a redirect URI of "https://…" with quote marks in it
# fails at Discord's consent screen. Leave the quotes off unless you are certain
# only Compose reads the file.
#
# One process, one origin. The image runs the NestJS API and nothing else, and
# that API serves the built Vue SPA itself out of apps/api/client
# (apps/api/src/main.ts). There is no nginx sidecar any more, so the SPA and
# /api share a single origin and the session cookie is same-origin by
# construction. A reverse proxy (Caddy, Traefik, nginx) in front terminates TLS
# and forwards to the container's port 3000.

# ===========================================================================
# Required — the API refuses to boot without these
# ===========================================================================
#
# "Refuses to boot" is exact, and narrow: all four keys below are read with
# ConfigService.getOrThrow(), which throws only when the key is *absent*. Present
# but empty (`DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=`) passes the check, so the container starts with
# a blank client ID and the failure surfaces later, at Discord's consent screen.
# Fill these in rather than leaving the blank templates from this file in place.

# From the Discord Developer Portal, General Information page.
DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=
DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Signs the session cookie. Use a long random value; rotating it logs everyone
# out. Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`. No quotes, and no characters
# that need shell quoting — see the note at the top of this file.
SESSION_SECRET=

# Path, not a full URL — keep it that way. Discord matches a redirect URI
# against the portal's registered list character for character (scheme, host,
# port and path), so a hardcoded origin breaks the moment you reach the app on a
# different one. Given a value with no scheme, discord-strategy resolves it
# against the origin of the incoming request instead (passport-oauth2's
# originalURL), so this one line covers the SPA's dev origin, a container
# published on any port, and kachna.cz.
#
# Each ORIGIN you log in from still has to be registered once, on the portal's
# OAuth2 page — that part is Discord's rule and no config avoids it:
#   http://localhost:5174/api/auth/discord/callback    (npm run dev:web)
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/discord/callback    (plain docker run)
#   https://kachna.cz/api/auth/discord/callback        (production)
# A missing entry fails at the consent screen with error 31013 before the request
# ever reaches us.
#
# Note the /api prefix: the API mounts every route under it (app.setGlobalPrefix
# in apps/api/src/main.ts), so the handler this points at is
# GET /api/auth/discord/callback. Requires app.set('trust proxy', 1) — set in
# main.ts — when a proxy terminates TLS in front of us, otherwise the derived
# origin comes out http:// and the token exchange is sent a URI that doesn't
# match the one authorize() used.
DISCORD_CALLBACK_URL=/api/auth/discord/callback

# ===========================================================================
# Required at first login (not at boot)
# ===========================================================================

# Only read inside the OAuth callback, when a profile is checked for guild
# membership — so a missing value boots fine and then rejects every login.
#
# SU FIT Discord guild ID = 369526274433089546
DISCORD_GUILD_ID=369526274433089546

# ===========================================================================
# Deployment
# ===========================================================================

# Set this in production. It is not cosmetic — apps/api/src/main.ts reads it
# twice: only with NODE_ENV=production does the API serve the built SPA (and the
# Vue Router history-mode fallback behind it, so deep links like /auth reload
# correctly), and only without
# it does the API demand FRONTEND_URL and install a CORS policy. Start the
# container without it and every frontend route 404s.
#
# The image does not set it, so put it in the env file or the compose service.
# Keep it out of any build stage: npm treats NODE_ENV=production as --omit=dev
# and would starve the build of typescript, vite and @nestjs/cli.
#
# Comment this line out if you copy this file somewhere the dev server reads —
# with it set, the API stops demanding FRONTEND_URL and tries to serve
# apps/api/client, which does not exist outside the image.
NODE_ENV=production

# Port the API listens on. The image EXPOSEs 3000, so leave this alone unless
# the EXPOSE line and the proxy's upstream change with it.
#PORT=3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Behind a reverse proxy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The API runs app.set('trust proxy', 1) — one hop, the proxy in front of it — so
# `req.protocol` and `req.host` follow X-Forwarded-Proto / X-Forwarded-Host
# instead of reading http:// and the loopback address. That is what makes a
# relative DISCORD_CALLBACK_URL resolve to the browser's real origin, and what
# makes the login popup's postMessage target come out https:// on a TLS site.
#
# Consequences for the proxy side:
#   - it must set those headers (Caddy's reverse_proxy and nginx with the usual
#     proxy_set_header lines both do);
#   - it must be the ONLY thing that can reach the container, because the last
#     hop is trusted by definition — anyone who can POST to the container
#     directly can otherwise assert any X-Forwarded-Host they like.

# ===========================================================================
# Development — mostly belongs in apps/api/.env instead
# ===========================================================================
#
# `npm run dev:api` and `npm run dev:web` run as two processes. Ports are fixed
# by convention: the API takes 5173 (PORT here, which is also vite.config.ts's
# `/api` proxy target), the SPA is pinned to 5174 in vite.config.ts. Everything
# the browser touches is therefore 5174 — including the OAuth round trip, which
# reaches the API through Vite's proxy — so 5174 is the origin to register:
#   http://localhost:5174/api/auth/discord/callback
#
# This file then needs nothing OAuth-specific for dev beyond the same relative
# DISCORD_CALLBACK_URL as everywhere else. An older setup registered 5173
# instead; that works only while the browser also sits on 5173, which it no
# longer does, and it strands the login popup's postMessage on the wrong origin.

# Required whenever NODE_ENV is not production — the API throws at boot without
# it, because that is when it installs the CORS policy. To be meaningful it has
# to be the exact origin the browser shows for the SPA, scheme included (so
# http://localhost:5174 in the layout above), but in practice any value satisfies
# the boot check: Vite proxies /api server-side, so the browser never makes a
# cross-origin request and the policy never fires. That is why the value of 5173
# in a working apps/api/.env is not a bug you need to chase.
#
# It used to double as the postMessage target for the login popup. It no longer
# is: the popup derives its target from the incoming request instead, so this
# only gates CORS. See the trust-proxy note above for what that implies.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:5174

# ===========================================================================
# No longer read by anything
# ===========================================================================
# Left here so a stale copy of this file in a deployed env file isn't a mystery.
#
#   DISCORD_SCOPES   — hardcoded to `identify guilds` in discord.strategy.ts
#   DISCORD_USE_PKCE — not passed to the strategy at all; the portal's "Web"
#                      application type authenticates with client_secret
#   PUBLIC_ORIGIN    — superseded; see the trust-proxy note above
